    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Tawantinsuyu, the largest pre-Columbian state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, stretched some 4,000 km from southern Colombia to central Chile.</li><li>It ran on labour tribute, storehouses, a 40,000 km road network and khipu records, without money or alphabetic writing.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">Smallpox</a> and a civil war between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huascar">Huáscar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atahualpa">Atahualpa</a> left it open to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a>’s conquest in 1532–1533.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Inca officials recorded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> figures, tribute and stored goods on khipus, knotted cords whose knot types and positions encoded numbers in a base-ten system.</p></aside>
    </section>

