    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/manco-capac"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manco-capac.jpg" alt="Manco Capac" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/manco-capac">Manco Capac</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Manco_Capac,_First_Inca,_1_of_14_Portraits_of_Inca_Kings.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Incas began as one of many small ethnic groups in the southern Peruvian highlands, centred on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a> Valley, where a local kingdom took shape from around the thirteenth century. Their origin myths tell of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manco-capac">Manco Capac</a> and his siblings emerging from the cave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacaritambo">Pacaritambo</a>, or from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-titicaca">Lake Titicaca</a>, to found Cusco. The transformation into an empire is traditionally dated to about 1438, when the Chanka, a powerful confederation from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andahuaylas">Andahuaylas</a> region, attacked Cusco. According to the chronicles, the aged ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viracocha-inca">Viracocha Inca</a> abandoned the city, but his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pachacuti">Cusi Yupanqui</a> defended it, defeated the Chanka and took the throne as Pachacuti. This date comes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-howland-rowe">John Rowe</a>’s reading of the Spanish chronicler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-cabello-de-balboa">Miguel Cabello de Balboa</a>; radiocarbon dates from imperial sites suggest that Inca expansion may have begun several decades earlier, so the familiar chronology is best treated as approximate. Pachacuti rebuilt Cusco as an imperial capital, subdued the neighbouring peoples of the highlands and the Lake Titicaca basin, and created the institutions of the new state. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupac-inca-yupanqui">Túpac Inca Yupanqui</a>, first as general and from about 1471 as ruler, conquered the Kingdom of Chimor on the north coast and pushed the frontiers into Ecuador and south into Chile, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huayna-capac">Huayna Capac</a>, from about 1493, extended rule to the north.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/coricancha"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/coricancha.jpg" alt="Coricancha" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/coricancha">Coricancha</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coricancha,_Cusco,_Per%C3%BA,_2015-07-31,_DD_68.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Diego Delso, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The empire, known to its people as Tawantinsuyu, the four parts together, may have held around ten million people, although estimates vary widely. Society rested on the <em>ayllu</em>, a kin-based community that held land collectively and shared labour, and on the Andean ideal of reciprocity. The state claimed rights over land, dividing it among the Inca state, the religion and the communities. Instead of taxes in goods or money, households owed labour, the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mita">mita</a></em>: they worked state and temple fields, served in armies, built roads and buildings, and spun and wove. Its produce filled thousands of storehouses, or <em>qollqa</em>, which fed soldiers, officials and workers and supplied the state’s generosity at feasts. Specialists stood outside the normal system: the <em>yanakuna</em>, retainers bound to the nobility and the state, and the <em>aqllakuna</em>, chosen women who wove fine cloth and brewed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> beer, or <em>chicha</em>. Cloth was among the most valued goods. Agriculture combined maize grown on irrigated terraces with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potatoes</a>, quinoa and other crops of the high plateau, alongside herds of llamas and alpacas. Long-distance markets played a small role compared with redistribution by the state. Religion centred on the sun god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inti">Inti</a>, honoured at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coricancha">Coricancha</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a>, alongside the creator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viracocha">Viracocha</a>, local sacred places called <em>huacas</em>, and the mummies of past rulers.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tupac-inca-yupanqui"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tupac-inca-yupanqui.jpg" alt="Túpac Inca Yupanqui" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tupac-inca-yupanqui">Túpac Inca Yupanqui</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tupac-inca-yupanqui-small.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Felipe Huaman Puma de Ayala, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Inca expansion relied on large armies raised through the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mita">mita</a></em> and fed from the storehouses along the roads, which allowed campaigns over great distances in difficult terrain. Commanders combined force with diplomacy: local lords were offered gifts and favoured status if they submitted, and those who resisted faced conquest and sometimes the resettlement of their people. The decisive victory over the Chanka around 1438 opened the way for conquests in the central and southern highlands, including the Colla and Lupaca kingdoms around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-titicaca">Lake Titicaca</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupac-inca-yupanqui">Túpac Inca Yupanqui</a> defeated the Kingdom of Chimor around 1470 and campaigned south through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/collasuyu">Collasuyu</a> into present-day Chile and north-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>, where the advance stopped near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maule-river">Maule River</a> in the face of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mapuche">Mapuche</a> resistance. In the north, the Cañari and the peoples of highland Ecuador were subdued only after hard fighting, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huayna-capac">Huayna Capac</a>’s campaign against the Caranqui ended in a massacre traditionally remembered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yahuarcocha">Yahuarcocha</a>, the Lake of Blood. The Incas built fortresses on contested frontiers, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inkallajta">Inkallajta</a> in the east and a chain of hilltop forts facing the peoples of the Amazonian lowlands. Near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a>, the great walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sacsayhuaman">Sacsayhuamán</a>, built of fitted stone blocks, served ceremonial as well as defensive purposes. Soldiers fought with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sling">slings</a>, maces, clubs, spears and bolas, but had no iron weapons, horses or firearms.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pachacuti"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pachacuti.jpg" alt="Pachacuti" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pachacuti">Pachacuti</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Pachacuti,_Tenth_Inca,_1_of_14_Portraits_of_Inca_Kings_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sapa-inca">Sapa Inca</a>, the unique Inca, was regarded as a son of the sun and ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a> with near-absolute authority, supported by the royal nobility. Each dead ruler’s mummy was kept and served by his descendants, organised in a royal kin group or <em>panaqa</em> that held his estates; these groups became powerful factions in politics. The empire was divided into four quarters, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinchaysuyu">Chinchaysuyu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antisuyu">Antisuyu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/collasuyu">Collasuyu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuntisuyu">Cuntisuyu</a>, under high officials, and further into provinces under governors. Below them the population was counted and organised in decimal units, from groups of 10 households up to 10,000, led by local lords, the <em>kurakas</em>, who were usually confirmed in office if loyal. Records of people, tribute and stores were kept on <em>khipus</em>, knotted cords read by specialists called <em>khipukamayuq</em>. A road network of some 40,000 kilometres, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qhapaq-nan">Qhapaq Ñan</a>, joined the empire, with way stations, or <em>tambos</em>, and relay runners, the <em>chaskis</em>, who carried messages quickly between them. The state spread <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a> as a common administrative language and moved whole communities as <em>mitmaqkuna</em>, settling loyal groups in unstable areas and relocating rebellious ones. Royal estates such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/machu-picchu">Machu Picchu</a>, probably built for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pachacuti">Pachacuti</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ollantaytambo">Ollantaytambo</a> combined residence, worship and agriculture. Succession, however, had no fixed rule, and every transfer of power risked open conflict among royal sons and their kin groups.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/machu-picchu"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/machu-picchu.jpg" alt="Machu Picchu" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/machu-picchu">Machu Picchu</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Machu_Picchu,_2023_(012).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Draceane, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In less than a century the Incas united a larger area and population than any other state of the pre-Columbian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, stretching from southern Colombia to central Chile, and integrated coastal, highland and forest peoples into one administrative system without money, markets on a large scale or alphabetic writing. Its roads, terraces, storehouses and masonry, built of stones fitted without mortar, remain among the most impressive works of pre-industrial engineering, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qhapaq-nan">Qhapaq Ñan</a> was inscribed on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage List</a> in 2014, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/machu-picchu">Machu Picchu</a> had been in 1983. Spanish rule in the Andes was built on Inca foundations. Colonial officials adapted the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mita">mita</a></em> to supply forced labour to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potosi">Potosí</a>, used <em>kurakas</em> as intermediaries, and relied on Inca roads and administrative divisions. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a>, spread by the Incas and later used by missionaries, is today spoken by millions of people in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>, Ecuador and neighbouring countries. Memory of the Incas inspired later resistance, from the Neo-Inca State at Vilcabamba to the great rebellion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupac-amaru-ii">Túpac Amaru II</a> in 1780. Accounts by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-cieza-de-leon">Pedro Cieza de León</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-betanzos">Juan de Betanzos</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/felipe-guaman-poma-de-ayala">Felipe Guaman Poma de Ayala</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-garcilaso-de-la-vega">Garcilaso de la Vega</a>, whose <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-commentaries-of-the-incas">Comentarios Reales de los Incas</a></em> appeared in 1609, made the Incas known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inti-raymi">Inti Raymi</a> is still celebrated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/huayna-capac"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/huayna-capac.jpg" alt="Huayna Capac" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/huayna-capac">Huayna Capac</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hp_inka11.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Felipe Huaman Puma de Ayala, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The empire fell at the height of its extent. Around 1527, while in the north, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huayna-capac">Huayna Capac</a> died suddenly of an epidemic disease, probably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a>, which had spread from European settlements ahead of the Spaniards themselves; his designated heir is said to have died too. Two sons claimed the throne: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huascar">Huáscar</a>, recognised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atahualpa">Atahualpa</a>, backed by the veteran armies in the north around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a>. The resulting civil war devastated the empire. Atahualpa’s generals <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quizquiz">Quizquiz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcuchimac">Chalcuchímac</a> defeated Huáscar’s forces in 1532 near Cusco and captured him, and Atahualpa’s troops killed many of Huáscar’s relatives and supporters. At this moment <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a> landed with fewer than 200 Spaniards. On 16 November 1532, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cajamarca">Cajamarca</a>, they ambushed Atahualpa’s escort, killed many of his attendants and took him prisoner. Atahualpa offered a room filled with gold and twice over with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> for his release, and had Huáscar killed in captivity. After the ransom was paid, the Spaniards executed him in July 1533. With the help of Andean peoples hostile to the Incas, Pizarro entered Cusco in November 1533 and installed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manco-inca">Manco Inca</a> as a puppet ruler. Manco rebelled in 1536, besieged Cusco, and withdrew to found the Neo-Inca State at Vilcabamba, which survived until the Spanish captured and executed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupac-amaru">Túpac Amaru</a> in 1572.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Inca Empire was the largest state of the pre-Columbian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, built within a century by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pachacuti">Pachacuti</a> and his successors and held together by labour tribute, roads, storehouses and knotted-cord records; weakened by epidemic and civil war, it fell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a>’s small Spanish force between 1532 and 1533.</em></p>

